## 1. Starting endpoint and implication chain

The canonical Target B sufficient closure is
\[
G+E\ge (\sqrt3/4)B+(\sqrt3/2)(t+\chi-1),
\]
strictly reversed for a global minimizer. The attempted lattice lane was

```text
uniform convex-lattice hull-vertex bound
  -> too few diameter pins for a dense two-direction unit-contact graph
  -> nonzero pinned expansive cone
  -> exclude exact nontriangular-lattice sources from the jammed branch.
```

The last arrow is proved. It does not pay the shell inequality or eliminate
the flexible branch, so it does not close Target B or Level I.

## 2. Uniform lattice theorem

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; noncanonical.

Let \(\Lambda\) be any planar lattice whose shortest nonzero vector has
length at least one, and assume that \(\Lambda\) is not a unit triangular
lattice. Let \(A\subset\Lambda\) be finite with \(n\ge2\).
Write \(n=|A|\), \(D=\operatorname{diam}A\), \(Q\) for all endpoints of
diameter pairs, \(q=|Q|\), and \(e\) for the number of unit contacts.
Then
\[
q\le b(A)\le 4(L+1/2)^2+\frac{\pi D}{L}
\quad(L>0),\qquad
e\le2n-2\sqrt n.                           \tag{2.1}
\]
where \(b(A)\) is the number of convex-hull vertices. In particular
\(q=O(D^{2/3})\), with an absolute constant independent of the lattice.
The pinned contact-preserving linear space has dimension at least
\[
2(n-q)-e\ge2\sqrt n-2q.                  \tag{2.2}
\]
If \(\sqrt n>q\), there is a nonconstant, same-diameter,
unit-separated straight-line motion of \(A\) that fixes every diameter
endpoint. If the unit-contact graph is connected, that motion can be chosen
to strictly reduce its contact count for all sufficiently small positive
times.

Consequently, for sufficiently large \(n\), **no global minimizer contained
in one unit-separated nontriangular lattice is in the canonical
first-order-jammed branch**. Such a minimizer with a connected contact graph
cannot be the
contact-minimal representative among global minimizers of that cardinality.
The conclusion includes every source point, including contact isolates; no
rhombic-disk, phase, or fixed-angle assumption is used.

### Proof

First classify the unit vectors of \(\Lambda\). Any two distinct unit
vectors are separated by at least one, so their angular gap is at least
\(\pi/3\). There are therefore at most six. If there are six, all gaps
equal \(\pi/3\), and two adjacent vectors generate a unit triangular
sublattice \(\Lambda_0\). Its unit equilateral-triangle tiling has covering
radius \(1/\sqrt3<1\). Since \(\Lambda_0\subseteq\Lambda\), any
point of \(\Lambda\setminus\Lambda_0\) would be within distance less than
one of some point of \(\Lambda_0\), contradicting the shortest-vector
assumption. Thus six unit vectors imply \(\Lambda=\Lambda_0\), excluded by
hypothesis. Central symmetry leaves either zero, two, or four unit vectors.

If there are four, write them \(\pm u,\pm v\). Each contact-graph component
lies in a coset of \(\mathbb Zu+\mathbb Zv\) and is a subgraph of its
horizontal/vertical grid. For coset \(k\), let \(n_k\) be its source
population and \(r_k,c_k\) its occupied coordinate-line counts. The path
count in each direction gives
\[
e_k\le(n_k-r_k)+(n_k-c_k)
\le2n_k-2\sqrt{n_k},
\]
because \(n_k\le r_kc_k\). Summing and using
\(\sum_k\sqrt{n_k}\ge\sqrt{\sum_k n_k}=\sqrt n\) yields
\(e\le2n-2\sqrt n\). If there are only two unit vectors, the contact graph
is a union of paths and isolated vertices, so \(e\le n-1\le2n-2\sqrt n\).
If there are none, \(e=0\). This proves the contact bound in (2.1).

Every endpoint of a diameter pair is a vertex of \(K=\operatorname{conv}A\):
if \(p\) is diametral from \(z\), then the linear functional
\((p-z)\cdot x\) has \(p\) as its unique maximizer on \(A\). Indeed, for
\(x\ne p\), equality with \(p\) would imply
\(|x-z|^2=D^2+|x-p|^2>D^2\).
Hence \(q\le b(A)\).

List the hull vertices in cyclic order. Because a convex polygon has strictly
turning edge directions after collinear boundary points are omitted, its
oriented edge vectors are distinct nonzero vectors of \(\Lambda\).
If the hull is a segment, it has only two vertices and the asserted bound is
immediate, so assume it is a polygon.
The lattice has minimum separation one. Disjoint radius-\(1/2\) disks about
its vectors of length below \(L\) lie in the radius-\(L+1/2\) disk, so there
are at most \(4(L+1/2)^2\) such vectors. The number of remaining hull edges
is at most \(\operatorname{per}(K)/L\le\pi D/L\), by Cauchy's perimeter
formula. This proves the first inequality of (2.1). Taking \(L=D^{1/3}\)
gives \(q\le b(A)=O(D^{2/3})\) uniformly in the lattice.

Pin the \(q\) diameter endpoints and impose zero first derivatives on all
\(e\) unit-contact lengths. This homogeneous linear system has
\(2(n-q)\) scalar unknowns and at most \(e\) independent equations, so
its kernel has dimension at least (2.2). If \(\sqrt n>q\), choose a nonzero
kernel velocity \(w\). For a contact \(ij\),
\[
|p_i+t w_i-p_j-t w_j|^2=1+t^2|w_i-w_j|^2\ge1.
\]
Every initially noncontact pair has strict separation slack, and every
initially nondiametral pair has strict diameter slack. Finiteness gives a
common sufficiently small positive \(t\) for which all those inequalities
persist. Every active diameter pair has both endpoints pinned, so its length
remains \(D\); all distances remain at most \(D\). This is a nonconstant
same-diameter feasible motion. If the contact graph is connected, some
contact has \(w_i\ne w_j\): otherwise all velocities on the graph would be
equal, and a pinned endpoint would make them all zero. That contact becomes
strictly longer than one, while no new contact appears for small \(t\).
Thus the contact count decreases. Any triangle-free source stays triangle-free
under this small motion, since no new unit contact is made.

Finally, the canonical strict translated-triangular-lattice comparison for a
global minimizer gives \(D^2<(2\sqrt3/\pi)(n-1)\). Thus
\(\sqrt n>D/\sqrt{2\sqrt3/\pi}\), while \(q=O(D^{2/3})\). For all
sufficiently large \(D\), \(\sqrt n>q\). The pinned expansive cone is
therefore nonzero, excluding the first-order-jammed branch. In the connected
case the motion yields another global minimizer with fewer contacts, excluding
contact minimality. \(\square\)

### Scope and dependencies

The rank and motion proof is self-contained. The only canonical imported
global input is the strict comparison in Target B Section 36; the definition
of the first-order-jammed branch is in shared local-structure Section 7.
No theorem from the unreviewed PRs #575, #576, #578, or #580 is used. The
argument works for arbitrary finite subsets of the one lattice, including
holes, disconnected contact graphs, and contact-isolated points. It proves
flexibility and, under connected contacts, loss of contacts. It does not
construct a configuration of diameter strictly below \(D(n)\).

## 3. Frontier effect and exact remaining lemma

The result removes exact unit-separated nontriangular-lattice sources from the entire
first-order-jammed branch without a pure-disk or fixed-angle hypothesis. It
does not change the canonical Target B shell inequality, because a flexible
global minimizer is not ruled out, and near-lattice points need not satisfy
the grid contact count. The exact next source-level lemma for this lane is:

> For all sufficiently large \(n\), if a triangle-free global minimizer is
> contained in one unit-separated nontriangular lattice, then there exists an
> \(n\)-point unit-separated configuration of diameter **strictly less**
> than \(D(n)\).

Even that lemma would eliminate only the exact-lattice lane; a Level I proof
must cover nonlattice sources and the Target A/C branches.

## 4. Regression and failure audit

| Attempt or regression | Outcome | Exact reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Infer global contradiction from nonzero pinned cone | Fails | The motion keeps all active diameter pairs at length \(D\). |
| Infer contact-count loss without connected contacts | Fails | An isolated source point can move with no contact to lose. |
| Extend (2.1) to arbitrary bounded-displacement charts | Fails | Perturbed points can acquire new contact directions; the grid edge count no longer applies. |
| Allow an ambient lattice with vectors shorter than one | Not covered | The source may omit short lattice pairs, and the unit-vector classification of the ambient lattice fails. |
| Treat a local jam or same-diameter flex as global optimality | Avoided | Globality is used only for the strict triangular comparison and the contact-minimal consequence. |
| Omit isolated source points | Avoided | They remain in \(n\) and in the linear system. |
| PR #580 irrational/near-resonance diameter-pair lemma | Different scope | The present hull count is uniform in all strict angles and needs no rationality assumption, but gives a weaker sublinear rate. |

No external theorem or computation is imported. Promotion recommendation:
**unreviewed** pending an independent proof audit. The state lock and active
route map are unchanged.

## 5. Handoff

The proved candidate is the uniform \(O(D^{2/3})\) diameter-pin bound and
the resulting grid-rank flex. The dependency chain is Target B Section 36
plus shared local-structure Section 7. The missing step is a strict
same-cardinality diameter competitor for exact-lattice flexible minimizers,
and then a source-level transfer beyond one lattice. The disk-clipped
tiny-shear sources in PR #580 show that scalar near-disk and shell-window
inequalities do not supply that step.


